Title:
Detection Chokepoints: LSASS Credential Dumping (T1003.001)

Type:
Documentation

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This write-up breaks LSASS credential dumping into three “invariant” detection stages (handle acquisition, memory read, credential extraction) that attackers must satisfy regardless of tool or evasion.  
- It focuses on kernel-enforced realities (e.g., needing an LSASS handle via NtOpenProcess and reading memory via NtReadVirtualMemory/MiniDumpWriteDump) and maps them to concrete telemetry sources.  
- Primary observables are Sysmon Event ID 10 (ProcessAccess) with GrantedAccess masks and CallTrace fingerprints (dbgcore/dbghelp for MiniDumpWriteDump, UNKNOWN for direct syscalls), plus Sysmon EID 11 (dump file creation) and EID 7 (SSP DLL load).  
- It catalogs many common variants (Mimikatz, comsvcs.dll MiniDump, ProcDump, nanodump, HandleKatz, PPL bypass via BYOVD, snapshotting via PssNtCaptureSnapshot, etc.) and explains how they still hit the same chokepoints.  
- The page includes Sigma rules at research/hunt/analyst maturity levels and a safe emulation script to generate telemetry without extracting credentials.  
- Useful primarily for Blue Teams/detection engineers and IR hunters, with value to red teams for understanding what is difficult to evade and where bypasses (e.g., handle duplication) shift signals.

Technical Focus:
- Sysmon EID 10 ProcessAccess (GrantedAccess masks, CallTrace analysis)
- LSASS handle acquisition via NtOpenProcess and kernel ObRegisterCallbacks
- Memory dumping/reading via MiniDumpWriteDump, NtReadVirtualMemory, direct syscalls
- Handle duplication evasion (NtDuplicateObject, PROCESS_DUP_HANDLE 0x0040)
- LSASS protections: PPL, Credential Guard (VBS), and BYOVD-based bypasses
- Sigma detection engineering (research vs hunt vs analyst rules)

Use Cases:
- Build/tune SIEM detections for LSASS dumping using Sysmon ProcessAccess telemetry
- Threat hunting for syscall-based dumpers (CallTrace=UNKNOWN) and LOLBin dumping (comsvcs.dll MiniDump)
- Validate detections in a lab using the provided emulation script (EID 10/1/11 generation)
- Baseline legitimate LSASS accessors (AV/EDR, WerFault, admin tools) to reduce false positives
- Investigate suspected credential theft by correlating LSASS access + dump artifacts + process creation

Keywords:
LSASS, T1003.001, Sysmon Event ID 10, ProcessAccess, GrantedAccess, CallTrace, NtOpenProcess, ObRegisterCallbacks, NtReadVirtualMemory, MiniDumpWriteDump, dbgcore.dll, dbghelp.dll, direct syscalls, NtDuplicateObject, PROCESS_DUP_HANDLE, comsvcs.dll MiniDump, ProcDump, Mimikatz, nanodump, HandleKatz, PPL, Credential Guard, BYOVD, Sysmon EID 11, Sysmon EID 7, Sigma rules, ETW Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence, Security Event ID 4656